menu "Board Support Package"

    config BSP_ERROR_CHECK
        bool "Enable error check in BSP"
        default y
        help
            Error check assert the application before returning the error code.

    menu "I2C"
        config BSP_I2C_NUM
            int "I2C peripheral index"
            default 0
            range 0 1
            help
                ESP32-S3 has two I2C peripherals, pick the one you want to use.
                The touch controller, IMU and RTC all share this bus.

        config BSP_I2C_FAST_MODE
            bool "Enable I2C fast mode"
            default y
            help
                I2C has two speed modes: normal (100kHz) and fast (400kHz).

        config BSP_I2C_CLK_SPEED_HZ
            int
            default 400000 if BSP_I2C_FAST_MODE
            default 100000
    endmenu

    menu "Display"
        config BSP_LCD_TRANS_QUEUE_DEPTH
            int "Depth of the QSPI transmission queue"
            default 10
            range 1 10
            help
                Cache the QSPI screen instruction/pixel data transmission tasks to implement
                asynchronous non-blocking screen refreshing, so the CPU can execute other logic
                after submitting a task.

        config BSP_DISPLAY_LVGL_BUF_HEIGHT
            int "LVGL draw buffer height in lines"
            default 12
            range 10 466
            help
                Height of each of the two LVGL draw buffers, in display lines. The buffers live
                in INTERNAL RAM, not PSRAM, because DMA reading PSRAM cannot keep the SPI FIFO
                fed while WiFi is also using PSRAM. Each costs hor_res * this * 2 bytes, so 20
                lines is about 11 KB per buffer and 22 KB for the pair.

                This trades directly against WiFi and TLS, both of which need internal RAM that
                only exists once. 40 lines (73 KB for the pair) starves esp_wifi_init(), which
                fails with ESP_ERR_NO_MEM and panics the board into a boot loop. 20 lines boots
                but leaves too little DMA-capable heap for the TLS handshake, so the aircraft
                fetch refuses to start and the radar shows NO MEM. Raise this only while
                watching both the startup heap log and /api/status.
    endmenu

endmenu
